<spell-notebook "author usage">
	<header level="1">
		Author
		<header>An author names who wrote a comment or did a feed event.</>
	</>
	<author-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#owners">Owners</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-author>` names who wrote a comment or did a feed event (Fomantic's comment `author` and feed `user`).

			- `link` renders a real link (`<a>`), e.g. to a profile; `target` goes with it.
			- In a feed it sits inline in the `<ui-summary>`; in a comment it opens the content, before the `<ui-meta>`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Owners">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-author>` finds its nearest owner and sets `:state(in-<owner>)` on itself; `UIParts.css` styles it by that
			state. Its owners, and what each makes of it:

			- [Comment](/components/ui/ui-comment.html) (`:state(in-comment)`): bold, at the start of the comment's content
			- [Feed](/components/ui/ui-feed.html) (`:state(in-feed)`): bold, inline in the event's summary

			Anywhere else it is an unstyled block. More on how parts find their owners: [Content parts](/components/ui/ui-header.html).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- With `link` it renders a real `<a>`: **Tab** reaches it, **Enter** follows it.
			- Without `link` it is plain inline text with no role.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
